    "keyNotes": "• Picture crafting is done on a rigid support: cartridge card at 250-350 gsm for studies, mount board 1.4-2 mm for mounting, 3 mm masonite (hardboard) and 4-6 mm plywood for finished panels.\n• Masonite has one smooth factory face and one mesh-imprinted back; mark the good face on the edge in pencil so nobody in the class paints on the wrong side.\n• Plywood and MDF carry glue and dust fumes, so sand at the extract point or outdoors, wear a mask, and never sand beside an open tin of solvent.\n• Adhesives are matched to the job: PVA (white wood glue) bonds paper to board, is water-based, dries clear and stays flexible; thin even coats stop the paper cockling.\n• Cassava starch paste is roughly one part flour to four parts water cooked to a clear gel; it is too weak for heavy objects but ideal for paper, and damp can reverse it.\n• Glair is egg white beaten and left to settle, sometimes with a drop of vinegar or clove oil to stop it souring; it is the traditional size for metal leaf and fine line work.\n• Contact cement is brushed on both faces, dried until tack-free, then pressed home once; a wrong join cannot be slid, so alignment is the whole skill (A1 quality).\n• A craft knife with a No. 11 blade cuts at about 45 degrees against a steel ruler; snap the blade off or change it the moment it drags instead of biting.\n• Cut away from the body on a self-healing mat, and turn the mat as you work so the blade never runs in an old groove and wanders.\n• Brushes: long handles for easel work, short for table work; hake (flat, 25-50 mm) for washes, filbert for soft edges, round for line, mop for blotting.\n• Never stand a brush bristles-up and never let glue dry in the ferrule; rinse in lukewarm water with mild soap, reshape the tip, then dry flat or handle-down.\n• Solvent work with turpentine, white spirit or methylated spirit needs cross ventilation, a strict no-smoking rule, and a lidded metal bin for oily rags, which can heat and ignite in a heap.\n• A palette of glass, sealed wood or disposable paper is cleaned before colour is mixed, because dried skin old colour dulls every new mixture.\n• Tidiness is marked: WASSCE Paper 3 pays for handling of materials, and tools returned to the rack with blades guarded, lids on and bench wiped are part of that handling.",
