          "content": "A support is the surface a picture is built on, and picture crafting uses a ladder of them. Paper for studies is judged in grams per square metre: ordinary bond is about 80 gsm and wrinkles at once, cartridge card at 250 to 350 gsm takes glue and light collage without buckling, and mount board, also called mattboard, at 1.4 to 2 mm gives a rigid, cuttable ground for a finished presentation. Above that come the boards: 3 mm masonite, a compressed hardboard with a smooth face on one side and a wire-mesh imprint on the other, is the standard school panel because it is cheap, flat and does not warp; 4 to 6 mm plywood is stronger and is used when the work will be hung in public or carries heavy objects. MDF is smooth and cuts cleanly but its dust is irritating, so it is sawn and sanded at the extract point outside the room.",
