    "isVip": false,
    "keyNotes": "• The seven elements are line, shape, form, tone, colour, texture and space; the principles of design are a separate list, so do not mix balance or rhythm into an element question.\n• A line is a point moved in a path: it measures, it describes an edge, and it can carry expression, so verticals read calm and upright, diagonals read movement, and jagged broken lines read tension.\n• Constructed line is drawn with ruler or compass for design work; expressive line is drawn from the shoulder in one confident sweep, and a hairy scratched outline loses both force and accuracy.\n• Shape is a closed two-dimensional area; if the outline fails to meet itself the shape escapes and the eye slides off, so closing the shape is the first discipline of the draughtsman.\n• Shapes are geometric (circle, square, triangle and their constructions) or organic (leaf, gourd, stone, cloud), and a picture gains interest when the two kinds are combined deliberately.\n• Form is shape with thickness: it is created by tone from one stated light source, by overlapping, or by contour lines that wrap round the volume.\n• Tone is the lightness or darkness of a colour; a nine-step value scale runs from paper white through middle grey to the darkest note the pencil or paint can give.\n• The tone families in a study object are highlight, half tone, form shadow, core shadow, reflected light, cast shadow and contact shadow; keep them in order or the sphere looks flat.\n• Colour is three facts at once, hue, value and intensity; a good picture controls all three, and mixing practice belongs to the next topic.\n• Texture is either actual, meaning tactile and really felt, or implied, meaning only suggested by mark-making, and both may sit on one board.\n• Space is the illusion of depth on a flat surface; overlapping, smaller size for distance, higher placement on the ground plane, less detail and paler tone in the far distance all create it.\n• Positive shape is the occupied area, negative shape is the air around and between the objects; drawing the negative shapes accurately is the fastest known cure for distorted proportion.\n• Isolation drill, the standard SHS 1 exercise: one A5 sheet where line alone is the subject, one where only tone appears, one where the same still life is built from negative shapes only.\n• A thumbnail is a 4 cm by 6 cm sketch that tests an arrangement of shapes, tones and focal point before real paint is mixed; make five before committing to one.",
