        "title": "Mixing a Sunlit Leaf Green and a Distant Olive for One Scene",
        "problem": "A roadside scene at Aburi needs two greens from the same foliage: a bright, warm sunlit green for the leaves near the picture front, and a greyed, cool green for the treeline in the distance. Choose the tubes and set out the mixing order.",
        "stepByStepSolution": [
          "Step 1 (M1): Lay out the tubes, lemon or hansa yellow, ultramarine blue, phthalocyanine blue, white, plus a small dish of red, the complement of green, for neutralising.",
          "Step 2 (M1): For the near sunlit green, mix lemon yellow with a touch of phthalocyanine blue, since both tubes lean towards green and neither carries red to dull the result.",
          "Step 3 (A1): Warm the near green with a knife point of yellow ochre or a trace of red so it reads sunlit and advances, and check it against the nine-step value scale at step 5 or 6.",
          "Step 4 (M1): For the distant treeline, mix a base green from yellow and ultramarine, accepting that the red in ultramarine already pushes the mixture towards olive.",
          "Step 5 (M1): Grey that base with a further trace of red, then cool and lighten it with white plus a hint of blue, so the mass sits back rather than jumping forward.",
          "Step 6 (A1): Compare the two greens on the same offcut under one daylight source; the near green must be higher in chroma and warmer, the far green lower in chroma, cooler and paler.",
          "Step 7 (A1): Record the tube names and order of addition beside each test so the mixture can be repeated across the whole panel."
        ],
        "keyTakeaway": "Tube lean decides a green, and the complement plus value plus temperature decide where that green sits in the picture."
