          "content": "Grattage is a scraping method. A ground, often dark, is laid and allowed to dry hard; a covering layer of crayon, wax, poster colour or thin tempera is then removed with a blade edge, an old identification card, the back of a knife or fine steel wool, so the pattern beneath flashes through. Success depends on timing, since a fully cured top layer fights the blade and a raw one simply smears, and on edge quality, because a dull scraper tears paper fibres instead of cutting film. Sgraffito means scratched, and the craft room handles it two ways. In the resist method a design is drawn firmly in wax crayon on a pale sheet, then a wash of dilute ink, tempera or water colour is floated over it; the wax repels the water, and the drawing appears in clean pale line against a toned ground. In the cutting method a light layer of poster colour or tempera is laid over a dark ground and, while still damp, the design is drawn through it with a stylus, a comb tooth, the nail-end of a brush handle or the edge of a maize cob, exposing the black below. Both methods teach economy, since a scratched line cannot be hidden.",
