          "content": "A texture board is a composed surface, and the syllabus expects a Ghanaian source. Adinkra symbols are ideal, since they were already made as relief: a pad carved from calabash or wood is padded in dye, so the mark carries a slight physical edge as well as colour. Choose two or three symbols with clear meanings, for example Adinkrahene for greatness, Dwennimmen for ram, horns and humility, and Nsoromma for the child of the heavens, and reduce them to flat silhouettes before any material is mixed. Plan the composition in a thumbnail, then build in three depth zones, low relief of sand or sawdust through glue for the ground, medium relief of cord, raffia or pressed tissue for the symbol edges, and one or two high points such as cowrie or beads at the focal area. Keep relief flat within about two centimetres of every edge so the panel can be mounted and packed, and dry the work under a light weight or flat on a board. Seal the whole surface once dry with PVA thinned about one part glue to one part water, or with a workable spray fixative held some 25 cm away in moving sweeps, then label the board with materials and methods used.",
