        "problem": "On a stretched quarter sheet of 300 gsm watercolour paper, paint a sky that fades from a deep blue at the top edge to pale at the horizon, with soft distant hills behind a crisp foreground. State the order of work.",
        "stepByStepSolution": [
          "Step 1 (M1): Confirm the sheet is dry and drum-tight on the tape, then pencil the horizon and the hill line very lightly so no groove is cut into the paper.",
          "Step 2 (M1): Wet the sky area with clean water using a large brush, and watch for the even gloss that tells the sheet is ready for wet-on-wet work.",
          "Step 3 (A1): Drop full-strength blue at the top edge and let it run, adding clean water to the brush after each pass so the passage grades from deep to pale in one descent.",
          "Step 4 (M1): While the field is still shining, lay a pale blue-grey band for the hills, then stop touching it, so the edges bloom into the sky and read as distance.",
          "Step 5 (A1): Leave the sheet undisturbed for about fifteen minutes; a re-watched wash is a scrubbed wash, and the paper fuzz will show in the final sheet.",
          "Step 6 (M1): Once the surface is dry to the touch, paint the foreground with mixed, firmer colour and a nearly dry brush so the trunks and figures keep hard edges.",
          "Step 7 (A1): Judge the sheet from two metres: the far band must be soft and pale, the near band crisp and darkest, and the paper white must still appear in the cloud breaks."
        ],
        "keyTakeaway": "Grading is done by adding water, not by adding white, and soft distance comes while the paper is still shining."
