          "content": "The adult head is an oval mass whose proportions can be checked in seconds, and the first check surprises most students: the eye line lies at about half the total head height, not near the top, so the cranium and forehead own the other half of the vertical space. The face below is read in thirds — hairline to brow ridge, brow ridge to base of nose, base of nose to chin — each third roughly equal in a front view. Across the front, the head is about five eye widths wide, and the ear fills the space between the brow line and the nose base. Rule these keys lightly in dilute burnt sienna before any tone goes down, because a tone laid on a wrong key has to be wiped off and the paper pays for it. When the model turns, the keys foreshorten together: the far ear moves toward the far cheek, the chin line tilts, and the same rules still govern everything you check.",
