      {
        "id": "ex-pc-mount-1",
        "title": "Sizing a window mount for a 40 cm by 30 cm gouache",
        "problem": "A finished gouache measures 40.0 cm wide by 30.0 cm high. It is to be window-mounted with 0.5 cm of board overlapping the image, 8.0 cm side and top borders, and a 9.5 cm tail border. Find the window opening size, the overall mount size, and the frame aperture needed.",
        "stepByStepSolution": [
          "Step 1 (M1): Subtract the overlap from both dimensions, since the mount lip must cover the cut edge: window width = 40.0 - (2 x 0.5) = 39.0 cm.",
          "Step 2 (A1): Window height = 30.0 - (2 x 0.5) = 29.0 cm, so the opening cut is 39.0 cm by 29.0 cm.",
          "Step 3 (M1): Add the borders to the opening, not to the picture: overall width = 39.0 + 8.0 + 8.0 = 55.0 cm.",
          "Step 4 (A1): Overall height = 29.0 + 8.0 (top) + 9.5 (tail) = 46.5 cm, giving a mount panel of 55.0 cm by 46.5 cm.",
          "Step 5 (M1): Mark the window position on the back first, squaring the 8.0 cm top border and confirming the tail is 9.5 cm, then cut the 45 degree bevel from the back in four single strokes with a fresh blade.",
          "Step 6 (A1): Cut backing board about 2 cm larger than the mount, that is 57 cm by 48.5 cm, and cut glazing 2 to 3 mm under the frame rebate so it drops freely.",
          "Step 7 (A1): State the frame aperture as 55 cm by 46.5 cm to the glazier, hinge the picture at the top only with acid-free paper hinges, and check the tail border looks visibly wider than the top border when sighted."
        ],
        "keyTakeaway": "Work from the window opening when adding borders, and buy the frame to the mount size, not to the picture size."
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-pc-frame-2",
        "title": "Stretching, finishing and hanging a 60 cm by 45 cm canvas",
        "problem": "A workroom stretcher measures 60 cm wide by 45 cm high with 25 mm bars. Plan the cutting size for canvas, the wiring, and the hook height so that the hung picture has its centre about 148 cm above the hall floor.",
        "stepByStepSolution": [
          "Step 1 (M1): Allow a 9 cm grip on every side, so cutting width = 60 + (2 x 9) = 78 cm.",
          "Step 2 (A1): Cutting height = 45 + (2 x 9) = 63 cm, giving a canvas piece of 78 cm by 63 cm; cut square to the weave so the threads run parallel to the bars.",
          "Step 3 (M1): Engage the middle of two opposite sides with canvas pliers and staple, then the other two middles, then work outward alternately; the printed focal area must stay centred and the weave square.",
          "Step 4 (M1): Size the cloth, then lay two thin gesso coats sanded between them, and tap the corner keys home once the primer is dry to restore tension.",
          "Step 5 (A1): Edge-coat the sides in the same colour as the sky, allow a full dry, then spray two light coats of satin acrylic varnish in moving air.",
          "Step 6 (M1): Fit two screw D-rings 15 cm in from each stile, giving a span of 60 - (2 x 15) = 30 cm, and cut wire to 30 + 4 = 34 cm with the slack looped upward.",
          "Step 7 (A1): The picture centre sits at half the height, 45 / 2 = 22.5 cm below the top edge, so with the centre at 148 cm the top edge lands near 170 cm above the floor; set the hook or plugged screw accordingly, level the work by sighting, and label the back in pencil."
        ],
        "keyTakeaway": "Cut to the weave, tension in sequence, protect the surface, then hang from a written centre-line height rather than by eye."
      }
