        "title": "Building a selling price for one mixed-media picture",
        "problem": "A student paints a 40 cm by 30 cm acrylic townscape of Adum. Materials cost GH¢ 126.00, taken as canvas board GH¢ 45.00, six tubes of paint at GH¢ 8.50 each and brushes GH¢ 30.00. The work took 22 hours and the written labour rate is GH¢ 9.00 an hour. Overheads are 15 percent and the skill margin is 20 percent. Find the quote.",
        "stepByStepSolution": [
          "Step 1 (M1): Verify the materials line: paint tubes 6 x GH¢ 8.50 = GH¢ 51.00, so materials = 45.00 + 51.00 + 30.00 = GH¢ 126.00.",
          "Step 2 (M1): Compute labour as hours multiplied by the written rate: 22 x GH¢ 9.00 = GH¢ 198.00.",
          "Step 3 (M1): Add the two direct costs: GH¢ 126.00 + GH¢ 198.00 = GH¢ 324.00, which is the prime cost of the work.",
          "Step 4 (M1): Add overheads at 15 percent of the prime cost: 324.00 x 0.15 = GH¢ 48.60, giving GH¢ 324.00 + GH¢ 48.60 = GH¢ 372.60.",
          "Step 5 (M1): Add the skill margin at 20 percent of that total: 372.60 x 0.20 = GH¢ 74.52, so the cost-recovery price is GH¢ 372.60 + GH¢ 74.52 = GH¢ 447.12.",
          "Step 6 (A1): Round the quote to a figure a buyer can read on a label, GH¢ 450.00, and write the full calculation on the cost sheet filed behind it.",
          "Step 7 (A1): State the profit position: at GH¢ 450.00 the student recovers GH¢ 447.12 of cost and keeps GH¢ 2.88 as rounding surplus, so any discount below GH¢ 447.12 begins to eat the margin."
        ],
        "keyTakeaway": "Materials, honest hours, overheads, margin, then round; the cost sheet is what lets you hold the price."
